1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Bb4 {is the line given in CHESS, August 1955, Vol.20/251, p439, but we don't know how the game proceeded until move 14. My suggestion is...} 4. e5 c5 5. a3 Bxc3+ 6. bxc3 Ne7 7. Qg4 Nf5 8. Bd3 Qa5 9. Bd2 h5 10. Qf4 Bd7 11. dxc5 Qxc5 12. Nf3 Nc6 13. Nh4 Nce7 14. O-O Qc7 $4 {This position occurred in the game, though the diagram prior to 14...Qc7 showed the BQ on a5. I can't see how the position can have occurred within the 14 moves stipulated. Now the game continues as per BH Wood's score...} 15. Nxf5 Nxf5 16. Bxf5 g5 17. Qg3 h4 18. Qxg5 exf5 19. Qf6 Rh7 20. Bg5 {"winning a second pawn when he wants it."... 31 moves. Source: Illustrated London News, 3 September 1955} 1-0

